WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.067 line:92%
-Our next guest represents

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.469 align:start size:94% position:6%
Maryland's
8th Congressional District

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:03.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
and was the lead
impeachment manager.

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:06.706
Please welcome to the show
Congressman Jamie Raskin.

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:08.274 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thanks for being here,
Congressman.

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:10.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm delighted to be with you,
Seth. Thanks for having me

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:14.848
-So, many were introduced to you
as the lead impeachment manager.

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:17.884 align:start size:94% position:6%
Many, from both sides
of the aisle,

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:19.586 size:88% position:44% line:92%
said you did a great job

00:00:19.586 --> 00:00:21.621 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that the defense
maybe didn't.

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:23.823 align:start size:94% position:6%
Were you surprised
by the preparation level

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:28.094 align:start size:94% position:6%
of Donald Trump's legal team
during the impeachment?

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:29.763 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Mm.

00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:31.831 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, let's see.
We don't speak ill

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.967 line:92%
of fellow members of the bar

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:43.410
but, when Mr. Castor finishe
that very long-winded opening,

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.444 line:92%
something ran through my mind,

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:48.681
that I'd heard in a scienc
class when I was in college.

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:52.786 align:start size:94% position:6%
A professor
who quoted a physicist

00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:55.388 align:start size:94% position:6%
who read someone else's pape
and said,

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:57.123 size:88% position:44% line:92%
"That's not even wrong."

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:57.624 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:57.624 --> 00:01:01.428
-Like I kind of felt like
he had never even addresse

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:04.330 size:88% position:44%
the issues in the case
or the facts of the case

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.133 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so it wasn't like
he got up and struck out.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.769 align:start size:94% position:6%
He just never
really found the stadium.

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:13.473
-Now, the one argument that ca
be made for that approach is

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:15.308 line:92%
he was probably fairly confident

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:18.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
that his client was not goin
to get impeached.

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:22.015 align:start size:94% position:6%
You went into it maybe a littl
more confident than that.

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:24.117
I have heard you say you thought
you were going to win

00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:29.789 align:start size:94% position:6%
with a unanimous Senate vote
100-0, voting to impeach.

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.624 line:92%
Voting to convict, I should say.

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:34.427
-Well, yeah, I thought tha
the evidence in the case

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:37.330 size:88% position:44% line:92%
was so overwhelming that

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:38.832 line:92%
a Senate that was doing its jo

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:40.633 align:start size:94% position:6%
of rendering
"impartial justice,"

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:43.636 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is the oath
that they swore to uphold,

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:46.673 size:81% position:41%
could do nothing other
than vote to convict.

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:49.742 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, as you say, he was alread
impeached by the House,

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:53.413
so we had the strongest
bipartisan majority in the House

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:55.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
in American history
for impeachment.

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:56.583 line:92%
And then, it came to the Senat

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:59.652 size:78% position:39%
and I thought that we
would get to 100-0,

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:03.389
that the bottom would just fal
out on the president's defense

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:05.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
but there were obviously peopl
who had decided,

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:09.162
at the beginning, that they were
going to vote to acquit hi

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:12.265 align:start size:94% position:6%
and hang their hat
on this technical defense,

00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:17.203 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he couldn't be tried
after leaving office,

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:21.241
even though he was impeached
for actions that he undertoo

00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:21.975 size:84% position:42% line:92%
while he was in office,

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:24.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
which has been the traditional
American approach,

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:26.779
going back to the very beginning
of the Constitution.

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:29.215 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Do you think that the results
would've been different

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:32.152
in the vote to convict,
had it been a blind vote,

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:33.820 align:start size:94% position:6%
where people
would not have known

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
how each senator
made their choice?

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:40.660 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, and a lot of Republicans
were saying that to me,

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.595 align:start size:94% position:6%
that, if they had made
the decision

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:45.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
to structure the rules
of the Senate differently,

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:47.167 line:92%
if it had been a secret ballot

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:50.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is basically what you've
got when a real jury meets

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:53.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
When a jury meets,
the public doesn't know

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:55.675 line:92%
who's voting in which directio

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:58.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so, if they had decide
to do the same thing,

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:00.313 size:78% position:39%
I think that would've
made a difference.

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:01.414 line:92%
I also kick myself, Seth,

00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:04.317
because I wish that I had moved,
at the beginning,

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.252 align:start size:94% position:6%
to do something they hadn'
even considered,

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:10.190
which was to keep them
from sitting in partisan blocks.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, they had
the Republicans

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:12.859 size:81% position:41% line:92%
over on the right side

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:14.727 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the Democrats
over on the left side.

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:17.597
They should've been seated
alphabetically or something else

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:20.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it encouraged them
to keep thinking of it

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:21.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
in partisan terms,
when the whole trick is

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:24.637 size:78% position:39%
to get them to render
an impartial verdict.

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:28.274
And that word, "impartial"
is a lot like "nonpartisan."

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:31.110 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Obviously, you know,
we're watching via the cameras

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.514 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you made
a very riveting case.

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:38.017
Did you feel as though you had
the attention of the senator

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:40.119 size:88% position:44% line:92%
when you were making it?

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:40.954 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-We had the attention

00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:43.356 align:start size:94% position:6%
of every senator
who was paying attention,

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:47.026
and the reason I say that is
because I looked out often

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:51.364 align:start size:94% position:6%
and saw not a majority,
at all, of the Republicans

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:53.099 line:92%
but a handful of Republicans

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:56.669 align:start size:94% position:6%
who were studiously refusing
to look at the camera

00:03:56.669 --> 00:04:01.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
when we showed footage
of the violent insurrection,

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.409 align:start size:94% position:6%
the attacks
on the police officers,

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:06.913 line:92%
the people chanting, you know,

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:08.681 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Hang Mike Pence!
Hang Mike Pence!

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:09.949 line:92%
Traitor! Traitor! Traitor!

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:12.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
They were essentially
closing their eyes to it

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:16.256 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, for that, I won't forgive
any of the senators.

00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:19.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, it's one thing
to get to the end and say,

00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:21.828 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I wasn't persuaded
by your case,"

00:04:21.828 --> 00:04:23.796 line:92%
or "I think jurisdictionally

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:27.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
even though this is
a radically novel argument

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:30.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
the president can't be tried
once he's left office,"

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:33.506 align:start size:94% position:6%
but it's quite another to say,
"I'm not even going to loo

00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:34.974 line:92%
at the facts of the case."

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:38.511 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
So, those people,

00:04:38.511 --> 00:04:41.214
those people, I'm not goin
to give for a long time.

00:04:41.214 --> 00:04:44.217
-Obviously, they probably
don't teach, in law school

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:46.052 line:92%
how to make a case to a jury

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:48.354 align:start size:94% position:6%
who are also witnesses
to the crime

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.690 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, one could even argue,
coconspirators.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:54.994
How do you approach laying out
a case to a roomful of peopl

00:04:54.994 --> 00:04:59.332 align:start size:94% position:6%
who were there
and saw it with their own eyes

00:04:59.332 --> 00:05:00.233 line:92%
-Well, you would've thought that

00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:02.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
that would make
our job a lot easier,

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:06.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it's like getting
a jury full of people

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:08.708 align:start size:94% position:6%
who were there
when the crime was committed

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:10.543 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the crime was committe
in the courtroom

00:05:10.543 --> 00:05:13.046
because we were meeting in one
of the places, you remember,

00:05:13.046 --> 00:05:15.648
that the insurrectionists
stormed and took over.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:18.418 align:start size:94% position:6%
They had their feet up
on all these people's desks.

00:05:18.418 --> 00:05:20.153 align:start size:94% position:6%
They were rifling
their belongings

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:22.855 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, if you've got any kin
of professional

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:24.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
or democratic self-respect
at all,

00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:27.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
you would think you'd go i
saying, you know,

00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:28.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
"The president's going
to pay for this,

00:05:28.728 --> 00:05:31.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
if he's the one
who got this thing started,"

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:32.732 line:92%
which, of course, he did.

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:37.637 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I was startled
that 43 of them

00:05:37.637 --> 00:05:41.274
were willing to just look
the other way and say,

00:05:41.274 --> 00:05:44.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
"No, this guy's got
too much power in our party,

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:47.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, even though,
if you're with Mitch McConnell

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:50.883
we made an overwhelming case
we proved our case on the facts,

00:05:50.883 --> 00:05:54.487 align:start size:94% position:6%
still, they were going to clai
that the president

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:56.889 align:start size:94% position:6%
could not be tried,
once he's left office,

00:05:56.889 --> 00:06:00.660
thereby creating what we wer
calling the January exception --

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.396 align:start size:94% position:6%
you can get away
with whatever you want,

00:06:03.396 --> 00:06:04.530 size:84% position:42% line:92%
once you hit January --

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.100 align:start size:94% position:6%
a coup, a rebellion,
an insurrection,

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.269 align:start size:94% position:6%
lining up members of Congress,
taking people hostage.

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:12.805
I mean, it's an absurdity,
when you think about it.

00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:16.175 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I quoted, you know,
Voltaire, who said,

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:18.845 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Anybody who can make you
believe absurdities

00:06:18.845 --> 00:06:20.546 line:92%
can make you commit atrocities."

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:22.115 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think
that captures pretty well

00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:24.817 align:start size:94% position:6%
the whole course of our histor
over the last few years.

00:06:24.817 --> 00:06:28.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, Donald Trump really wa
convincing people, somehow

00:06:28.521 --> 00:06:29.422 size:88% position:44% line:92%
he had won the election,

00:06:29.422 --> 00:06:31.891 align:start size:94% position:6%
when he lost by more
than seven million votes.

00:06:31.891 --> 00:06:34.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was a landslide,
on his own terms,

00:06:34.293 --> 00:06:37.530
in the Electoral College
and, yet, there are still people

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
walking around like zombie
in Congress today,

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
saying that Donald Trump
won the election.

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:44.971 line:92%
-Obviously, to pick up something

00:06:44.971 --> 00:06:47.340 align:start size:94% position:6%
like being
the impeachment manager,

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:49.308 align:start size:94% position:6%
that requires a great deal
of patriotism

00:06:49.308 --> 00:06:49.976 size:88% position:44% line:92%
to take a job like this.

00:06:49.976 --> 00:06:53.579
This was a time -- and we want
to pass along our condolences --

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:56.115 align:start size:94% position:6%
you tragically
lost your son last year

00:06:56.115 --> 00:06:59.152 align:start size:94% position:6%
and to take up
this very difficult job

00:06:59.152 --> 00:06:59.986 line:92%
that obviously required a lo

00:06:59.986 --> 00:07:02.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
of your time, focus,
and energy --

00:07:02.655 --> 00:07:04.857 align:start size:94% position:6%
was it important for you
to channel that grief

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:07.527 align:start size:94% position:6%
into something that mattered
as much as this?

00:07:07.527 --> 00:07:08.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think so.
My son Tommy,

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:13.099 align:start size:94% position:6%
who was in his second year
at Harvard Law School,

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:17.937
was a great champion of the rule
of law and the rule of law

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:21.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
as an instrument of justic
and democracy

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:23.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I felt him very much
next to my heart

00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:26.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
and in my chest
over the course of the trial

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:30.016
And, for me, it was important --
I just kept thinking,

00:07:30.016 --> 00:07:33.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I'm not going to lose my so
on the last day of 2020

00:07:33.853 --> 00:07:35.888 line:92%
and lose my country in 2021.

00:07:35.888 --> 00:07:38.224 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have got to establish
that this is

00:07:38.224 --> 00:07:42.361 size:78% position:39%
completely off-limits
conduct and behavior.

00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:46.098
We can never allow anything like
that to happen to us again -

00:07:46.098 --> 00:07:50.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
to have a president
who basically incites

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:54.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
a violent attack
to overturn an election,

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:56.943 align:start size:94% position:6%
in order to install himsel
in office.

00:07:56.943 --> 00:07:58.678 line:92%
And so I felt Tommy with me,

00:07:58.678 --> 00:08:02.281 align:start size:94% position:6%
as well as my my daughters
Hannah and Tabitha;

00:08:02.281 --> 00:08:03.249 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and Sarah, my wife;

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:05.618 align:start size:94% position:6%
my whole family;
and all of my colleagues.

00:08:05.618 --> 00:08:06.419 size:84% position:42% line:92%
And, as you know, Seth,

00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:10.356 align:start size:94% position:6%
we had just a remarkable tea
of impeachment managers

00:08:10.356 --> 00:08:13.426 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they will be my brothers
and sisters forever,

00:08:13.426 --> 00:08:16.128 align:start size:94% position:6%
after what we
went through there.

00:08:16.128 --> 00:08:18.331 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Well, we thank you

00:08:18.331 --> 00:08:20.800 align:start size:94% position:6%
for what you put
into the historical record

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.836 align:start size:94% position:6%
and thank you so much
for making time for us today

00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:25.238 size:78% position:39% line:92%
Really appreciate it.

00:08:25.238 --> 00:08:28.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I appreciate your having me
Seth. Best to you.

